When you use the @LoadBalanced annotation on a RestTemplate (or a RestClient.Builder), you are triggering a behind-the-scenes mechanism provided by Spring Cloud LoadBalancer (or previously Netflix Ribbon).
Here is exactly how the @LoadBalanced annotation and the local registry cache work together step by step:
## 1. The Interaction Flow

[ Eureka Server ]
       │
       ▼ (Every 30 seconds)
[ Order Service ] ──► Updates its [ Local Registry Cache ]
       │
       ▼ user calls /order/checkout
[ @LoadBalanced RestTemplate ]
       │
       ├─ 1. Intercepts "http://payment-service/..."
       ├─ 2. Looks up "payment-service" in the [ Local Registry Cache ]
       ├─ 3. Picks one healthy IP (e.g., 192.168.1.50:8081)
       │
       ▼ (Direct network call)
[ Payment Service Instance ]

## 2. What @LoadBalanced Actually Does
The @LoadBalanced annotation is a qualifier. It tells Spring to inject a special HTTP Client Interceptor into your RestTemplate.

* Without @LoadBalanced, a RestTemplate treats http://payment-service as a real domain name and tries to resolve it via DNS (which will fail with a UnknownHostException).
* With @LoadBalanced, the interceptor catches your request before it goes over the network. It parses the URL, strips away the virtual name (payment-service), and asks the local load-balancing library to resolve it.

## 3. What the "Local Cache" Means
Your order-service does not ping the Eureka Server every time an HTTP request is made. Doing so would create a massive performance bottleneck and flood the Eureka Server with traffic.
Instead:

* Background Sync: When your order-service starts up, it downloads the entire directory of available microservices from the Eureka Server and saves it in its own application memory.
* Heartbeat Updates: Every 30 seconds (by default), a background thread in your order-service pings Eureka to refresh this local memory cache with any updates (like new instances starting up or dead ones being removed).

## 4. Putting It Together
When your code executes:

restTemplate.getForObject("http://payment-service/payment/status", String.class);


   1. The @LoadBalanced interceptor halts the request.
   2. It looks at the local cache to find all IP addresses registered under "payment-service".
   3. It applies your chosen strategy (like Round-Robin) to pick an IP.
   4. It rewrites your request on the fly to something like http://192.168.1 and lets the network call proceed.

This is why it is called client-side load balancing—the decision of where to send the traffic happens entirely inside the memory of the order-service, using its local cache.
